{"type":"final","text":"Both variables are found, proven, recorded, and promoted.\n\n## Results\n\n**Pause flag: `$00E0`** (binary — `0` = playing, `1` = paused)\n- **Differential:** snapshot on the first overworld screen → pressed Select → snapshot showed `$00E0` 0→1 (the clean bit among the frame/scratch noise).\n- **Across pause/resume:** with `$00E0`=1, holding Right for 60 frames left Link's X (`$0070`) stuck at 120 — input ignored. Pressing Select again dropped `$00E0` to 0 and the same Right hold moved X 120→174.\n- **Causal proof by write:** from a clean state I wrote `$00E0`=1 → held Right 40 frames → `$0070` stayed 120 and the byte stayed 1 (game didn't overwrite it). Wrote `$00E0`=0 → held Right 30 frames → `$0070` climbed 121→160.\n- Note: the rendered screen is static while paused, so a screenshot alone can't confirm pause — the frozen-X test is the tell.\n\n**Controller 1 byte: `$00FA`** (bitmask: A=`$80`, B=`$40`, Select=`$20`, Start=`$10`, Up=`$08`, Down=`$04`, Left=`$02`, Right=`$01`)\n- **Found from code, confirmed by play:** traced reads of the controller port `$4016` (1920 reads/120 frames, all at PC `$E651`). Disassembly of the read routine at `$E640`–`$E678` shows it latching `$4016`, shifting 8 reads, and storing the merged state to `$00FA,X` (player 2 → `$00FB`); newly-pressed edges go to `$00F8`/`$00F9`.\n- **Watch proof:** one button at a time on `$00FA` — Right→1, Up→8, A→128, Select→32, Start→16, each held for the full press and returning to 0. Standard LSB-first order (A = bit 7, Right = bit 0).\n\n**One correction worth flagging:** an earlier session had recorded `$00FA` as a transient render copy of Link's facing direction. This session's watch shows it's the raw controller-1 state byte; it \"looks like\" a direction only because holding a D-pad sets that bit. Both new records note this.\n\n`promote_mappings` saved both to the ROM's mapping file (now 8 addresses). Nothing was written to ROM, so no patch/ledger changes."}
